WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:19.439
Our abduction took place so quickly that I shivered all over. Who were our captors?

2
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.701
They were undoubtedly some new sort of pirates, who explored the sea in their own way.

3
00:00:24.702 --> 00:00:29.844
The narrow hatch closed above us and we were surrounded by complete darkness.

4
00:00:29.845 --> 00:00:35.931
My eyes, dazzled by the brightness outside the vessel, couldn't adjust to the lack of light inside.

5
00:00:35.932 --> 00:00:41.169
The men forced us down a metal ladder. At the bottom of the ladder, a door opened.

6
00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:46.002
We were shoved into a room and the door immediately shut behind us with a bang.

7
00:00:46.003 --> 00:00:51.092
The three of us were alone, and I couldn't even begin to imagine where we were.

8
00:00:51.093 --> 00:00:55.592
My eyes were still not able to discern even the faintest glimmer of light.

9
00:00:55.593 --> 00:01:02.997
"These people are the worst hosts I've ever met!" shouted Ned. "Fortunately I still have my knife with me."

10
00:01:02.998 --> 00:01:06.489
"Calm yourself, my friend," said Conseil.

11
00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:12.402
"Yes, Ned," I said. "Don't make the situation worse with senseless violence.

12
00:01:12.403 --> 00:01:18.806
Perhaps we can get our captors to listen to us, but first let's try to figure out where we are."

13
00:01:18.807 --> 00:01:26.720
I began to grope my way around the room. In five steps I came to a wall made of iron plates bolted together.

14
00:01:26.721 --> 00:01:32.631
Turning around, I ran into a wooden table around which were arranged several stools.

15
00:01:32.632 --> 00:01:38.639
The floor of this prison was concealed under thick mats that deadened the sound of footsteps.

16
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:46.456
The bare walls revealed no trace of window or door. I guessed that the cabin measured about twenty feet by ten.

17
00:01:46.457 --> 00:01:52.699
Half an hour had passed without any change in our situation when suddenly a bright light went on.

18
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.283
I squeezed my eyes tight against the light.

19
00:01:56.284 --> 00:02:02.774
When I slowly opened them, I saw that the source of this brightness was an electric light in the ceiling.

20
00:02:02.775 --> 00:02:10.107
The sudden illumination of the cabin allowed me to examine it more carefully, but it only contained the table and five stools.

21
00:02:10.108 --> 00:02:15.179
We couldn't hear a sound; everything seemed dead onboard this mysterious vessel.

22
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:22.599
Was it floating on the surface of the sea or had it dived into the ocean's depths? I had no idea.

23
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:30.250
But then we did hear a sound: the noise of bolts sliding. A door opened in a wall and two men appeared.

24
00:02:30.251 --> 00:02:35.591
One was short and very muscular with thick black hair and a mustache.

25
00:02:35.592 --> 00:02:41.416
The second man was tall with a large forehead, straight nose, and fine teeth.

26
00:02:41.417 --> 00:02:46.813
One peculiar feature was that his cold, black eyes were set rather far apart.

27
00:02:46.814 --> 00:02:54.821
After observing him for a few moments, I decided that he was a calm, self-confident man full of energy and courage.

28
00:02:54.822 --> 00:03:00.313
But whether this man was thirty-five or fifty years of age, I could not say.

29
00:03:00.314 --> 00:03:07.218
The two strangers wore fur caps and boots, and their clothes were made from a fabric that I couldn't identify.

30
00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:13.218
The taller man, who was evidently the captain of the vessel, carefully examined us in silence.

31
00:03:13.219 --> 00:03:18.377
Then he said something to his lieutenant in a language that I couldn't understand.

32
00:03:18.378 --> 00:03:23.369
The second man replied to him and stared at me as if waiting for me to speak.

33
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:28.297
I said in French that I didn't know his language, but he didn't seem to understand me.

34
00:03:28.298 --> 00:03:35.948
"Master, if you were to tell our story, perhaps these gentlemen might understand some words," suggested Conseil.

35
00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:39.781
I began to describe our adventures onboard the Abraham Lincoln.

36
00:03:39.782 --> 00:03:49.011
Then I introduced us as Professor Pierre Aronnax, his servant Conseil, and Mr. Ned Land, the harpooner.

37
00:03:49.012 --> 00:03:55.273
The men listened to us quietly, but there was nothing in their faces to indicate that they'd understood a word.

38
00:03:55.274 --> 00:04:01.924
"Perhaps they understand English," I said to Ned. "You speak it better than I do. Give it a try."

39
00:04:01.925 --> 00:04:10.749
Once more Ned told our story in English, but with the same result. Next Conseil tried in German with no success.

40
00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:18.836
I even attempted to tell it in Latin. The two strangers exchanged some words in their unknown language and left the room.

41
00:04:18.837 --> 00:04:26.995
Ned was furious. "We spoke to those rogues in French, English, German, and Latin, and they didn't have the politeness to answer!"

42
00:04:26.996 --> 00:04:31.503
"Calm yourself, Ned," I said. "Anger won't do us any good."

43
00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:36.900
"But don't you see, Professor? We'll die of hunger in this iron prison!" he replied.

44
00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:42.979
"We won't starve," said Conseil calmly. "We can hold out for some time yet."

45
00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:52.241
"My friends, we must not despair," I said. "Meanwhile I can't decide if our captors are from Spain, Turkey, Arabia, or India.

46
00:04:52.242 --> 00:04:54.805
Their language is quite incomprehensible."

47
00:04:54.806 --> 00:05:00.384
As I said these words, the door opened and a steward entered with fresh clothes for us.

48
00:05:00.385 --> 00:05:03.805
While we hurried to dress, the steward set the table.

49
00:05:03.806 --> 00:05:06.630
"Now that's more like it!" said Conseil.

50
00:05:06.631 --> 00:05:11.630
"Bah!" said Ned. "What do you suppose they eat here—tortoise liver?"

51
00:05:11.631 --> 00:05:15.122
The place settings were elegant and in perfect taste.

52
00:05:15.123 --> 00:05:23.614
We were undoubtedly dealing with civilized people, and if it weren't for the electric light, I could imagine we were dining in one of the finest hotels in Europe.

53
00:05:23.615 --> 00:05:30.852
All the dishes and silverware were inscribed with the letter N and a Latin motto: Mobilis in mobili.

54
00:05:30.853 --> 00:05:36.106
"What does that mean, Professor?" asked Ned, running a finger over the letters.

55
00:05:36.107 --> 00:05:39.818
"N is undoubtedly the captain's initial," I said.

56
00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:47.098
"And the inscription means mobile within the mobile element or in this case moving through water."

57
00:05:47.099 --> 00:05:51.344
We didn't talk much after that, being too busy devouring our food.

58
00:05:51.345 --> 00:05:58.162
I recognized some fish dishes, but I wasn't sure if some of the other things we ate were animals or vegetables.

59
00:05:58.163 --> 00:06:01.662
At least our hosts were not going to let us starve.

60
00:06:01.663 --> 00:06:07.170
After eating, my two companions settled themselves on the floor of the cabin and were soon asleep.

61
00:06:07.171 --> 00:06:11.487
But I lay awake wondering where we were and with whom.

62
00:06:11.488 --> 00:06:15.654
At one point I thought the vessel was sinking to the bottom of the sea.

63
00:06:15.655 --> 00:06:20.987
Tormented by nightmares, I tossed and turned before finally falling into a deep sleep.

64
00:06:20.988 --> 00:06:24.488
When I awoke I didn't know how long we'd been sleeping.

65
00:06:24.489 --> 00:06:29.471
We must have used up most of the air in the cabin because it was difficult to breathe.

66
00:06:29.472 --> 00:06:36.304
Just as I was speculating about how the captain refreshed the air for the whole vessel, I smelled a sea breeze.

67
00:06:36.305 --> 00:06:41.877
The ship rolled, and I realized that it had risen to the surface to breathe like a whale.

68
00:06:41.878 --> 00:06:46.614
Ned and Conseil awoke and breathed in the pure, fresh air.

69
00:06:46.615 --> 00:06:49.289
"What time is it?" asked Ned.

70
00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:55.122
"I have no idea," I replied. "It's possible that we've slept for 24 hours."

71
00:06:55.123 --> 00:07:01.701
More time passed and Ned grew angry. There was no sound on the boat, and it didn't move.

72
00:07:01.702 --> 00:07:07.360
I felt terrified; Conseil remained calm; Ned roared with anger and hunger.

73
00:07:07.361 --> 00:07:13.693
Suddenly the door opened and the steward appeared. With a shout Ned threw the man to the floor.

74
00:07:13.694 --> 00:07:22.098
Conseil tried to pull Ned off the steward. I was just about to jump into the fray when I was stopped by hearing these words in French:

75
00:07:22.099 --> 00:07:30.147
"Be quiet, Mr. Land, and you, Professor Aronnax, will you kindly listen to me?"
